    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Rock strain is generally accommodated by brittle fracture (jointing and/or faulting) in the upper crust of the earth. This process maybe modeled using continuum and fracture mechanics principles. Here the growth of opening-mode fractures (joints) with lengths of meters to tens of meters is analyzed to understand the effect of propagation velocity on the final pattern. The fractures grow from randomly distributed flaws in an elastic material. Equilibrium fracture networks are generated numerically for constant remote strain loading conditions. The results show how the relative propagation velocities between fractures can exert a controlling influence on the ultimate network geometry. This model provides a powerful tool for the quantitative analysis brittle deformation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
